course: dictionary
lesson: 1689
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning the writer wants. The word «chronic» is a good example.
explain: «A chronic condition is an illness that lasts a long time or for life, like asthma or heart disease.» The clue is "lasts a long time or for life", so chronic means long-lasting. Doctors use this meaning when they talk about illnesses that can be controlled but not cured.
explain: «The film was chronic» is different. Here chronic is British slang for very bad. The clue is that a film is being judged, and films cannot have a long-lasting illness. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check: "The film was long-lasting" does not make sense, so the slang meaning is right.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, so always check the clues in the sentence.
fact: Chronic means lasting a long time or keeping on coming back.
fact: A chronic habit is one a person has had for a long time and cannot stop, like «a chronic liar».
fact: In British slang, «chronic» means very bad, as in «The film was chronic».
fact: Phrases to know are «chronic pain», «chronic illness», «a chronic shortage» and «chronically ill». Chronic illnesses, like asthma, are the opposite of acute ones, which come on suddenly and pass.
exercise: «A chronic condition is an illness that lasts a long time or for life.» What does chronic mean here? | Easy to cure in a week | Sudden and short | Very bad, like a bad film | *Long-lasting
exercise: Doctors say asthma is chronic. What does this tell you? | *It lasts a long time and can often be controlled but not cured | It is only a cold | It means the patient is a bad actor | It comes on suddenly and goes away quickly
exercise: Ben says «The film was chronic», so he thinks the film was great. What is wrong with Ben's idea? | Chronic means great, so Ben is right | Nothing is wrong | Chronic means long-lasting, so the film was long | *In this slang, chronic means very bad
test: «He is a chronic liar; he has lied every day for years.» What does chronic mean here? | A kind of illness that can be cured quickly | *Something a person has done for a long time and cannot stop | Very good at telling stories | Something that happens once and then stops
test: Which phrase from the lessons means a problem that never goes away? | A brief pause | A quick fix | *A chronic shortage | A sudden shower
exam: «The school dinners were chronic.» Which meaning fits best? | Healthy and tasty | Long-lasting, as an illness is | *Very bad, British slang | Sudden and short
